--TEST--
A `secret` compared against a `mixed` takes the constant-time comparison too, and answers what the ordinary one does — `rule:security/secret-comparison-is-constant-time` and `rule:expressions/mixed-equality`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:security/secret-comparison-is-constant-time` applies wherever *either*
// operand is `secret`, and the shape that reaches in practice is a credential
// compared against a value that arrived as `mixed` — a decoded request field, a
// header, a cache read. The comparison the neighbouring
// comparing-two-secrets-answers-the-same-as-comparing-two-strings.nvst pins has
// two buffers; this one has a buffer and a runtime tag, and the point is the
// same: the timing property is bought and the answer does not move, so every
// row below is one `rule:expressions/mixed-equality`'s short-circuiting row
// would answer identically.
secret string $expected = "s3cr3t-token";

mixed $correct = "s3cr3t-token";
mixed $wrong = "s3cr3t-tokeN";
mixed $shorter = "s3cr3t-toke";

if ($expected == $correct) { echo "equal\n"; } else { echo "BAD\n"; }
// Differs in the last byte alone: the row a short-circuiting comparison
// answers slowest, and the one the rule exists for.
if ($expected == $wrong) { echo "BAD\n"; } else { echo "last byte differs\n"; }
if ($expected == $shorter) { echo "BAD\n"; } else { echo "length differs\n"; }
// Which side carries the tag is not a property of the comparison.
if ($correct == $expected) { echo "equal either way\n"; } else { echo "BAD\n"; }
// `!=` is the same helper negated, one tag over.
if ($expected != $wrong) { echo "not equal\n"; } else { echo "BAD\n"; }
if ($expected != $correct) { echo "BAD\n"; } else { echo "not unequal\n"; }

// A tag with no buffer behind it is `false` rather than a throw, which is the
// answer the unqualified row gives the same pair.
mixed $number = 42;
mixed $nothing = null;
mixed $flag = true;
if ($expected == $number) { echo "BAD\n"; } else { echo "an int is not it\n"; }
if ($expected == $nothing) { echo "BAD\n"; } else { echo "null is not it\n"; }
if ($expected == $flag) { echo "BAD\n"; } else { echo "a bool is not it\n"; }

// `rule:types/bytes`: a `bytes` is a value rather than a handle and compares
// only against another `bytes`, so the same octets under the other tag are not
// equal — the one row a comparison reading the shared buffer alone would get
// wrong, since `string` and `bytes` are one allocation.
mixed $sameOctets = "s3cr3t-token" as bytes;
if ($expected == $sameOctets) { echo "BAD\n"; } else { echo "octets are not text\n"; }

// `rule:security/secret-qualifier` puts the qualifier on `bytes` too, and the
// rows hold one base over.
secret bytes $mac = "abc" as bytes;
mixed $sameMac = "abc" as bytes;
mixed $otherMac = "abd" as bytes;
mixed $asText = "abc";
if ($mac == $sameMac) { echo "bytes equal\n"; } else { echo "BAD\n"; }
if ($mac == $otherMac) { echo "BAD\n"; } else { echo "bytes differ\n"; }
if ($mac == $asText) { echo "BAD\n"; } else { echo "text is not octets\n"; }
--EXPECT--
equal
last byte differs
length differs
equal either way
not equal
not unequal
an int is not it
null is not it
a bool is not it
octets are not text
bytes equal
bytes differ
text is not octets
